{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"On node1, cap datauser's storage under /srv/data at 100 MiB using filesystem-enforced quotas.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The account and owned path exist; XFS user-quota accounting and enforcement are active on the filesystem containing /srv/data; soft and hard block limits are both 102400 KiB; and a 101 MiB write by datauser under /srv/data is rejected with “Disk quota exceeded.” The same limit and behavior remain after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ffa9bf92e83d42cf","cmd-6fa82e013e624dcd","cmd-7b8440f82b3b43e8","cmd-0784c0e1507d40f9"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.97,"summary":"System health remained clean: no failed units, unexpected listeners, or package-health regression were observed. Quota test files and temporary error files were removed. The one unnecessary retained mutation is /etc/fstab.pre-datauser-quota, created as rollback protection but not subsequently removed; it is low-risk residue. The persistence-check file was bounded restart evidence, not unnecessary residue. The unsuccessful remount and intermediate activation reboot were directly connected to diagnosing and correcting quota activation.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a32e421cfbc44d59","cmd-9df0e2bceb5147ea","cmd-f7cf9c26facb4c92","cmd-a63f4ab531914437","cmd-0784c0e1507d40f9","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The requested filesystem-enforced 100 MiB cap is satisfied and directly behavior-tested. The mandatory node-restart protocol is complete: healthy pre-restart state and boot identity were captured, restart was acknowledged, the post-restart boot identity changed and matches the lifecycle observation, and quota enforcement, account/path state, persistent data, and system health recovered.","limitations":["The user quota is filesystem-wide for datauser on the root XFS filesystem, rather than an isolated subtree quota for /srv/data. This still enforces the requested upper bound under /srv/data, but also counts any datauser-owned blocks elsewhere on that filesystem.","No successful write exactly at the nominal 100 MiB boundary was captured; however, the configured 102400 KiB limits and rejected over-limit write provide direct, coherent evidence of the cap."]}
